      </table>
      <p style="margin-top: 16px;"><strong>Key differences by origin:</strong></p>
      <p><strong>Australian Opals:</strong> More stable and durable, better suited for long-term astrological wear and Venus remedies, lower moisture sensitivity.</p>
      <p><strong>Ethiopian Opals:</strong> Visually striking and more budget-accessible, hydrophane in nature — absorb moisture, better suited to jewellery than long-term astrological use.</p>
      <p style="margin-top: 16px;">Beyond origin, price is shaped by intensity and variety of colour play, base tone (black and blue Opals are the rarest and most premium), surface finish, structural stability, and whether the stone is solid natural Opal or composite. As always: a smaller Australian Opal with vivid, shifting colour will almost always outperform a larger, dull, or unstable stone.</p>
      <div style="background: #fdfaf5; padding: 20px; border-left: 4px solid var(--color-gold); margin-top: 20px;">
        <p style="margin:0;"><strong>Ideal Weight:</strong> 6–7 ratti (approximately 5–6 carats).</p>
        <p style="margin:8px 0 0;"><strong>Best Metal:</strong> Silver or Panchdhatu traditionally. Gold when astrologically advised.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'ruby' -%}
