        <tr style="background: #fdfaf5;"><td style="padding: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;"><strong>Iran / Arizona</strong></td><td style="padding: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">Robin's egg blue; minimal matrix</td><td style="padding: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">₹500 – ₹10,000+ per carat</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="padding: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;"><strong>Tibet / China</strong></td><td style="padding: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">Greenish-blue with webbing</td><td style="padding: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;">₹200 – ₹2,000+ per carat</td></tr>
      </table>
      <div style="background: #fdfaf5; padding: 20px; border-left: 4px solid var(--color-gold); margin-top: 20px;">
        <p style="margin:0;"><strong>Note:</strong> Stability and origin transparency are key for Turquoise.</p>
        <p style="margin:8px 0 0;"><strong>Best Metal:</strong> Silver preferred for calm aesthetics</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'surya' -%}
      <p>Surya page pricing reflects the gemstones of the Sun — primarily Ruby (Manik). Ruby pricing is shaped above all by colour, treatment status, and origin, with Burma remaining the classic benchmark.</p>
      <p>Refer to our Ruby collection for detailed pricing by origin, colour quality, and treatment status. Ideal weight for astrological use is 2–7 carats in Gold.</p>
    {%- when 'chandra' -%}
      <p>Chandra page pricing reflects the gemstones of the Moon — primarily Pearl (Moti). Pearl pricing varies significantly by type: natural Basra Pearls are priced by weight and rarity, South Sea Pearls range from ₹500–₹50,000+ per carat, and Freshwater Pearls are more accessible.</p>
      <p>Lustre, nacre thickness, origin, and absence of treatment are the key value factors. Silver is the traditional metal for Moon gemstones.</p>
    {%- when 'mangal' -%}
      <p>Mangal page pricing reflects the gemstones of Mars — primarily Red Coral (Moonga). Italian Sardinian Red Coral ranges from ₹800–₹25,000+ per carat; Japanese Aka Coral is the premium choice at ₹4,000–₹40,000+.</p>
      <p>Colour uniformity, density, and natural origin are the primary value factors. Gold or Copper is the traditional metal for Mars gemstones.</p>
    {%- when 'budh' -%}
      <p>Budh page pricing reflects the gemstones of Mercury — primarily Emerald (Panna). Pricing ranges widely by origin, from Brazilian stones at ₹1,500+ to Colombian Emeralds at ₹8,000–₹10,00,000+ per carat.</p>
      <p>Colour vibrancy, treatment level, clarity balance, and origin are the key value factors. Gold is the traditional metal for Mercury gemstones.</p>
    {%- when 'shukra' -%}
      <p>Shukra page pricing reflects the gemstones of Venus — primarily White Sapphire and Opal. White Sapphire from Sri Lanka ranges ₹2,000–₹1,50,000+ per carat; Australian Opal ranges ₹600–₹50,000+ per carat.</p>
      <p>Clarity of light, treatment transparency, and natural origin are the key value factors. Gold or Silver is the traditional metal for Venus gemstones.</p>
    {%- when 'guru' -%}
      <p>Guru page pricing reflects the gemstones of Jupiter — primarily Yellow Sapphire (Pukhraj). Sri Lanka and Burma stones range ₹1,800–₹3,50,000+ per carat; Madagascar offers strong value at ₹1,600–₹2,00,000+.</p>
      <p>Colour warmth, treatment status, and colour stability over time are the key value factors. Gold is the traditional metal for Jupiter gemstones.</p>
    {%- when 'shani' -%}
      <p>Shani page pricing reflects the gemstones of Saturn — primarily Blue Sapphire (Neelam). Kashmir stones command ₹4,00,000+ per carat; Ceylon ranges ₹1,500–₹3,00,000+; Burma commands ₹2,000–₹2,50,000+.</p>
      <p>Colour depth, treatment status (untreated preferred), and origin are the key value factors. Silver or Panchdhatu is the traditional metal for Saturn gemstones.</p>
    {%- when 'rahu' -%}
      <p>Rahu page pricing reflects the gemstones of Rahu — primarily Hessonite (Gomed). Sri Lanka Hessonite ranges ₹1,200–₹55,000+ per carat; African Hessonite ₹900–₹35,000+.</p>
      <p>Honey-amber colour with inner glow is the most valued quality. Avoid dark, muddy, or dull tones. Silver is the traditional metal for Rahu gemstones.</p>
    {%- when 'ketu' -%}
      <p>Ketu page pricing reflects the gemstones of Ketu — primarily Chrysoberyl Cat's Eye (Lehsunia). Chrysoberyl ranges ₹2,000–₹50,000+ per carat; Quartz Cat's Eye is far more affordable at ₹300–₹3,000+.</p>
      <p>Sharpness of the eye, contrast, and base mineral type (Chrysoberyl only for astrological use) are the key value factors. Silver is the traditional metal for Ketu gemstones.</p>
    {%- when 'iolite' -%}
      <p>Iolite is a moderately priced semi-precious gemstone, generally ranging from ₹200–₹3,000+ per carat depending on colour saturation, clarity, and origin. Deep violet-blue with strong pleochroism commands the highest prices.</p>
      <p>It is an accessible alternative to more expensive Saturn or Rahu stones. Silver or white metals are commonly preferred settings.</p>
    {%- when 'red-garnet' -%}
      <p>Red Garnet is widely available and generally ranges from ₹200–₹5,000+ per carat for natural specimens, with premium Mozambique or Burmese material commanding higher prices. Colour intensity, clarity, and cut are the primary value factors.</p>
      <p>It is among the most accessible Mars-aligned gemstones, making it a practical entry point for those seeking Mars energy support. Gold or Silver settings are both suitable.</p>
    {%- when 'peridot' -%}
      <p>Peridot ranges from ₹300–₹8,000+ per carat depending on the depth of its yellow-green colour, clarity, and size. Pakistani Peridot from the Himalayan region is considered among the finest and commands premium prices.</p>
      <p>Rich, deeply saturated olive-green stones are the most valued. Silver is the commonly preferred metal setting for Peridot.</p>
    {%- when 'tourmaline' -%}
      <p>Tourmaline pricing varies significantly by colour variety: green and pink tourmalines are widely available at ₹300–₹10,000+ per carat, while Paraiba Tourmaline — the rarest variety — commands ₹50,000–₹10,00,000+ per carat.</p>
      <p>Colour saturation, clarity, and rarity of variety are the primary value factors. Setting metal choice depends on the specific colour variant and intended purpose.</p>
    {%- when 'white-coral' -%}
      <p>White Coral pricing is similar to Red Coral and ranges from ₹500–₹10,000+ per carat for natural specimens. Natural, untreated White Coral is preferred for any astrological use, and treatment status must always be fully disclosed.</p>
      <p>Colour uniformity, surface quality, and natural origin are the key value factors. Gold or Silver is the typical setting metal.</p>
    {%- when 'agate' -%}
      <p>Agate is one of the most widely accessible gemstones, generally ranging from ₹50–₹2,000+ per carat for natural specimens. Premium varieties such as Fire Agate or rare banded patterns command higher prices.</p>
      <p>Pattern quality, colour vibrancy, and natural origin are the primary value factors. Silver or metal settings complement Agate's earthy qualities well.</p>
    {%- when 'chalcedony' -%}
      <p>Chalcedony ranges from ₹100–₹3,000+ per carat depending on colour, translucency, and variety. Blue Chalcedony is particularly prized for its calm, even colour and silky translucency.</p>
      <p>Colour evenness, translucency, and natural quality are the primary value factors. Silver is the commonly preferred setting metal for Chalcedony.</p>
    {%- when 'pitambari' -%}
      <p>Pitambari Sapphire is a rare and premium stone, generally starting at ₹5,000+ per carat and reaching ₹1,00,000+ per carat for fine specimens with clearly defined dual colour zones and excellent clarity. Its rarity makes pricing highly variable.</p>
      <p>The quality and distribution of the yellow-blue colour combination, treatment status, and origin are the key value factors. Gold is the traditional setting metal for Pitambari.</p>
    {%- when 'aquamarine' -%}
      <p>Aquamarine ranges from ₹500–₹20,000+ per carat depending on colour depth, clarity, and size. Deep Santa Maria blue is the most prized variety, while lighter sky-blue stones offer excellent value at accessible prices.</p>
      <p>Colour depth, absence of treatment (most fine aquamarine is untreated), and clarity are the key value factors. Silver or white gold are commonly preferred setting metals.</p>
    {%- when 'natural-zircon' -%}
      <p>Natural Zircon ranges from ₹500–₹15,000+ per carat for colourless or blue varieties. It is important to insist on certification confirming natural Zircon — not cubic zirconia or glass, which are far cheaper and have no gemological or astrological value.</p>
      <p>Brilliance, fire, clarity, and natural origin are the key value factors. Gold or white metal settings are preferred for Venus gemstone use.</p>
    {%- when 'moonstone' -%}
      <p>Moonstone ranges from ₹200–₹15,000+ per carat depending on the quality of adularescence (the floating blue glow), base tone, and clarity. Rainbow Moonstone with strong blue flash commands the highest prices.</p>
      <p>Strength and quality of the adularescence, body transparency, and origin are the key value factors. Silver is the traditional setting metal for Moon gemstones.</p>
    {%- when 'lapis-lazuli' -%}
      <p>Lapis Lazuli ranges from ₹200–₹5,000+ per carat depending on colour uniformity, pyrite distribution, and origin. Afghan Lapis is considered the global benchmark for deep, even royal blue with harmonious pyrite inclusions.</p>
      <p>Colour uniformity, pyrite quality (evenly distributed gold specks preferred), and natural origin are the key value factors. Gold or Silver settings both complement Lapis well.</p>
    {%- when 'malachite' -%}
      <p>Malachite ranges from ₹200–₹3,000+ per carat for quality natural specimens. Congo Malachite is considered the most prized for its vivid green colour and beautifully defined banding patterns.</p>
      <p>Banding quality, colour depth, and natural origin are the key value factors. Malachite is softer than crystalline gems and should be set protectively. Gold or copper-toned metals complement its colour well.</p>
    {%- when 'sphatik' -%}
      <p>Sphatik (clear quartz) is one of the most accessible natural materials, ranging from ₹50–₹2,000+ per carat for clear specimens. Sphatik Shivalinga are priced based on size, clarity, and craftsmanship rather than per-carat weight.</p>
      <p>Absolute clarity, absence of cracks, and natural (not synthetic or glass) origin are the key value factors for astrological or spiritual use.</p>
    {%- when 'blue-topaz' -%}
      <p>Blue Topaz is widely available and ranges from ₹200–₹5,000+ per carat for natural specimens. Swiss Blue and London Blue are the most valued varieties for their depth and richness of colour.</p>
      <p>Colour depth, clarity, and cut quality are the primary value factors. Silver or white gold is the most commonly preferred setting metal for Blue Topaz.</p>
    {%- when 'tiger-eye' -%}
      <p>Tiger's Eye is one of the more accessible gemstones, ranging from ₹100–₹2,000+ per carat for quality specimens. Premium stones with sharp, well-defined chatoyancy and rich golden-brown colour command higher prices.</p>
      <p>Sharpness of the chatoyancy, colour richness, and natural origin are the key value factors. Gold or brass-toned settings complement Tiger's Eye's warm colour naturally.</p>
    {%- when 'purple-sapphire' -%}
      <p>Purple Sapphire ranges from ₹1,500–₹50,000+ per carat depending on colour richness, treatment status, and origin. Fine untreated specimens with vivid, even violet-purple colour are the most valuable and increasingly rare.</p>
      <p>Colour saturation, treatment status, and origin are the key value factors. Gold or white gold are commonly used setting metals for Purple Sapphire.</p>
    {%- when 'padparadscha' -%}
      <p>Padparadscha Sapphire is among the rarest and most valuable sapphire varieties, generally ranging from ₹20,000–₹5,00,000+ per carat for certified natural specimens with the classic pink-orange sunset colour. Fine untreated stones command exceptional premiums.</p>
      <p>The precise balance of pink and orange colour, treatment status (untreated strongly preferred), origin, and clarity are the key value factors. Gold is the traditional setting metal.</p>
    {%- when 'star-sapphire' -%}
      <p>Star Sapphire ranges from ₹2,000–₹1,00,000+ per carat depending on the sharpness and definition of the six-rayed star, body colour, transparency, and origin. Fine Burma or Ceylon blue Star Sapphires with sharp, centred stars command the highest prices.</p>
      <p>Star sharpness and centering, body colour, and natural origin are the key value factors. Silver or white metal is traditionally preferred for Saturn gemstones including Star Sapphire.</p>
  {%- endcase -%}
